mod 16$: $149 \\equiv 149 - 9 \\times 16 = 149 - 144 = 5 \\pmod{16}$. So $149^{150} \\equiv 5^{150} \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$5^2 = 25 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$. $5^4 \\equiv 81 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$. So $5^{150} = 5^{4 \\times 37 + 2} = (5^4)^{37} \\cdot 5^2 \\equiv 1 \\cdot 9 = 9 \\pmod{16}$.\n\nSo $149^{150} \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$149^{150} \\mod 625$: $149 \\equiv 149 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$\\phi(625) = 625 \\times 4/5 = 500$. Since $\\gcd(149, 625) = 1$, $149^{500} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^{150} \\mod 625$. Let me compute this step by step.\n\n$149^2 = 22201$. $22201 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 35 = 21875$. $22201 - 21875 = 326$. So $149^2 \\equiv 326 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^4 \\equiv 326^2 = 106276 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 170 = 106250$. $106276 - 106250 = 26$. So $149^4 \\equiv 26 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^8 \\equiv 26^2 = 676 \\pmod{625}$. $676 - 625 = 51$. So $149^8 \\equiv 51 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^{16} \\equiv 51^2 = 2601 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 4 = 2500$. $2601 - 2500 = 101$. So $149^{16} \\equiv 101 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^{32} \\equiv 101^2 = 10201 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 16 = 10000$. $10201 - 10000 = 201$. So $149^{32} \\equiv 201 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^{64} \\equiv 201^2 = 40401 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 64 = 40000$. $40401 - 40000 = 401$. So $149^{64} \\equiv 401 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^{128} \\equiv 401^2 = 160801 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 257 = 160625$. $160801 - 160625 = 176$. So $149^{128} \\equiv 176 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$149^{150} = 149^{128} \\times 149^{16} \\times 149^4 \\times 149^2 \\equiv 176 \\times 101 \\times 26 \\times 326 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$176 \\times 101 = 17776$. $17776 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 28 = 17500$. $17776 - 17500 = 276$. So $176 \\times 101 \\equiv 276 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$276 \\times 26 = 7176$. $7176 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 11 = 6875$. $7176 - 6875 = 301$. So $276 \\times 26 \\equiv 301 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$301 \\times 326 = 98126$. $98126 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 157 = 98125$. $98126 - 98125 = 1$. So $301 \\times 326 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nSo $149^{150} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nNow CRT: $149^{150} \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$ and $149^{150} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nFind $x$ such that $x \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$ and $x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$, $0 \\le x < 10000$.\n\n$x = 625k + 1$. Need $625k + 1 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$, i.e., $625k \\equiv 8 \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$625 \\mod 16 = 625 - 39 \\times 16 = 625 - 624 = 1$. So $k \\equiv 8 \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$k = 8$: $x = 625 \\times 8 + 1 = 5001$.\n\nCheck: $5001 \\mod 16 = 5001 - 312 \\times 16 = 5001 - 4992 = 9$. ✓. $5001 \\mod 625 = 5001 - 8 \\times 625 = 5001 - 5000 = 1$. ✓.\n\nSo $149^{150} \\equiv 5001 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nNow, $149^{150} \\mod 1510000$. We have $149^{150} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$ and $149^{150} \\equiv 5001 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nCRT: $x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$, $x \\equiv 5001 \\pmod{10000}$, $0 \\le x < 1510000$.\n\n$x = 10000k + 5001$. Need $10000k + 5001 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$10000 \\mod 151$: $151 \\times 66 = 9966$. $10000 - 9966 = 34$. So $10000 \\equiv 34 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$5001 \\mod 151$: $151 \\times 33 = 4983$. $5001 - 4983 = 18$. So $5001 \\equiv 18 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$34k + 18 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$, i.e., $34k \\equiv -17 \\equiv 134 \\pmod{151}$.\n\nNeed inverse of $34 \\mod 151$. $34^{-1} \\mod 151$.\n\n$151 = 4 \\times 34 + 15$. $34 = 2 \\times 15 + 4$. $15 = 3 \\times 4 + 3$. $4 = 1 \\times 3 + 1$. $3 = 3 \\times 1$.\n\nBack: $1 = 4 - 1 \\times 3 = 4 - 1 \\times (15 - 3 \\times 4) = 4 \\times 4 - 15 = 4 \\times (34 - 2 \\times 15) - 15 = 4 \\times 34 - 9 \\times 15 = 4 \\times 34 - 9 \\times (151 - 4 \\times 34) = 40 \\times 34 - 9 \\times 151$.\n\nSo $34^{-1} \\equiv 40 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$k \\equiv 40 \\times 134 \\pmod{151}$. $40 \\times 134 = 5360$. $5360 \\mod 151$: $151 \\times 35 = 5285$. $5360 - 5285 = 75$. So $k \\equiv 75 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$k = 75$: $x = 10000 \\times 75 + 5001 = 750001$.\n\nSo $149^{150} \\equiv 750001 \\pmod{1510000}$.\n\nThen $\\frac{149^{150} - 1}{151} = \\frac{750001 - 1}{151} = \\frac{750000}{151}$. Wait, but this should be an integer. $750000 / 151$... let me check: $151 \\times 4966 = 151 \\times 5000 - 151 \\times 34 = 755000 - 5134 = 749866$. That's not 750000.\n\nHmm, let me recheck. $149^{150} \\equiv 750001 \\pmod{1510000}$, and $149^{150} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$. Check: $750001 \\mod 151$. $151 \\times 4966 = 749866$. $750001 - 749866 = 135$. That's not 1!\n\nLet me recheck my CRT computation.\n\n$x = 10000k + 5001$, $x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$10000k + 5001 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$10000 \\equiv 34 \\pmod{151}$. $5001 \\equiv 18 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$34k + 18 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{151} \\Rightarrow 34k \\equiv -17 \\equiv 134 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$k \\equiv 40 \\times 134 \\pmod{151}$. $40 \\times 134 = 5360$. $5360 / 151 = 35.5...$. $151 \\times 35 = 5285$. $5360 - 5285 = 75$. $k \\equiv 75 \\pmod{151}$.\n\n$x = 10000 \\times 75 + 5001 = 755001$. Wait, I made an arithmetic error. $10000 \\times 75 = 750000$. $750000 + 5001 = 755001$.\n\nLet me recheck: $755001 \\mod 151$. $151 \\times 5000 = 755000$. $755001 - 755000 = 1$. ✓\n\n$755001 \\mod 10000 = 5001$. ✓\n\nSo $149^{150} \\equiv 755001 \\pmod{1510000}$.\n\n$\\frac{149^{150} - 1}{151} \\mod 10^4$: $\\frac{755001 - 1}{151} = \\frac{755000}{151} = 5000$.\n\nSo $\\frac{149^{150}-1}{151} \\equiv 5000 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nLet me verify: $151 \\times 5000 = 755000$. $755000 + 1 = 755001 \\equiv 149^{150} \\pmod{1510000}$. ✓\n\nSo $q_1 \\mod 10^4 = 5000$.\n\n**Computing $\\frac{151^{148}-1}{149} \\mod 10^4$:**\n\nI need $151^{148} \\mod 1490000$ where $1490000 = 149 \\times 10000$.\n\n$151^{148} \\mod 149 = 1$ (Fermat).\n\n$151^{148} \\mod 10000$: Use CRT with $10000 = 16 \\times 625$.\n\n$151 \\mod 16 = 151 - 9 \\times 16 = 151 - 144 = 7$. So $151^{148} \\equiv 7^{148} \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$7^2 = 49 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$. So $7^{148} = (7^2)^{74} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$151^{148} \\mod 625$: $151 \\equiv 151 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$\\phi(625) = 500$. $151^{500} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$151^{148} \\mod 625$.\n\n$151^2 = 22801$. $22801 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 36 = 22500$. $22801 - 22500 = 301$. So $151^2 \\equiv 301 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$151^4 \\equiv 301^2 = 90601 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 144 = 90000$. $90601 - 90000 = 601$. $601 - 625 = -24 \\equiv 601 \\pmod{625}$. Wait, $601 < 625$, so $151^4 \\equiv 601 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nHmm wait, let me recompute. $625 \\times 144 = 90000$. $90601 - 90000 = 601$. Yes, $151^4 \\equiv 601 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$151^8 \\equiv 601^2 = 361201 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 577 = 361125$. $361201 - 361125 = 76$. So $151^8 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$151^{16} \\equiv 76^2 = 5776 \\pmod{625}$. $625 \\times 9 = 5625$. $5776 - 5625 = 151$. So $151^{16} \\equiv 151 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nInteresting! $151^{16} \\equiv 151 \\pmod{625}$. This means $151^{15} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$ (since $\\gcd(151, 625) = 1$).\n\nLet me verify: $151^{16} \\equiv 151 \\pmod{625}$ means $151 \\times (151^{15} - 1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{625}$. Since $\\gcd(151, 625) = 1$ (151 is prime, doesn't divide 625), $151^{15} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nSo the order of 151 mod 625 divides 15. Let me check: $151^{15} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$148 = 15 \\times 9 + 13$. So $151^{148} = (151^{15})^9 \\times 151^{13} \\equiv 1^9 \\times 151^{13} = 151^{13} \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$151^{13} = 151^{8} \\times 151^{4} \\times 151^{1} \\equiv 76 \\times 601 \\times 151 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$76 \\times 601 = 45676$. $45676 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 73 = 45625$. $45676 - 45625 = 51$. So $76 \\times 601 \\equiv 51 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$51 \\times 151 = 7701$. $7701 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 12 = 7500$. $7701 - 7500 = 201$. So $151^{13} \\equiv 201 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nSo $151^{148} \\equiv 201 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nCRT: $151^{148} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$ and $151^{148} \\equiv 201 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$x = 625k + 201$. Need $625k + 201 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$625 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$. $201 \\mod 16 = 201 - 12 \\times 16 = 201 - 192 = 9$.\n\n$k + 9 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16} \\Rightarrow k \\equiv -8 \\equiv 8 \\pmod{16}$.\n\n$k = 8$: $x = 625 \\times 8 + 201 = 5201$.\n\nCheck: $5201 \\mod 16 = 5201 - 325 \\times 16 = 5201 - 5200 = 1$. ✓. $5201 \\mod 625 = 5201 - 8 \\times 625 = 5201 - 5000 = 201$. ✓.\n\nSo $151^{148} \\equiv 5201 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nNow CRT for $151^{148} \\mod 1490000$: $x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{149}$, $x \\equiv 5201 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\n$x = 10000k + 5201$. Need $10000k + 5201 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{149}$.\n\n$10000 \\mod 149$: $149 \\times 67 = 9983$. $10000 - 9983 = 17$. So $10000 \\equiv 17 \\pmod{149}$.\n\n$5201 \\mod 149$: $149 \\times 34 = 5066$. $5201 - 5066 = 135$. So $5201 \\equiv 135 \\pmod{149}$.\n\n$17k + 135 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{149} \\Rightarrow 17k \\equiv -134 \\equiv 15 \\pmod{149}$.\n\nNeed $17^{-1} \\mod 149$.\n\n$149 = 8 \\times 17 + 13$. $17 = 1 \\times 13 + 4$. $13 = 3 \\times 4 + 1$. $4 = 4 \\times 1$.\n\nBack: $1 = 13 - 3 \\times 4 = 13 - 3 \\times (17 - 13) = 4 \\times 13 - 3 \\times 17 = 4 \\times (149 - 8 \\times 17) - 3 \\times 17 = 4 \\times 149 - 35 \\times 17$.\n\nSo $17^{-1} \\equiv -35 \\equiv 114 \\pmod{149}$.\n\n$k \\equiv 114 \\times 15 \\pmod{149}$. $114 \\times 15 = 1710$. $1710 \\mod 149$: $149 \\times 11 = 1639$. $1710 - 1639 = 71$. So $k \\equiv 71 \\pmod{149}$.\n\n$k = 71$: $x = 10000 \\times 71 + 5201 = 715201$.\n\nCheck: $715201 \\mod 149$. $149 \\times 4799 = ?$. Let me compute $715201 / 149$. $149 \\times 4800 = 715200$. $715201 - 715200 = 1$. ✓\n\n$715201 \\mod 10000 = 5201$. ✓\n\nSo $151^{148} \\equiv 715201 \\pmod{1490000}$.\n\n$\\frac{151^{148} - 1}{149} = \\frac{715201 - 1}{149} = \\frac{715200}{149} = 4800$.\n\nSo $q_2 \\mod 10^4 = 4800$.\n\n**Final answer:**\n\n$q_1 + q_2 \\mod 10^4 = 5000 + 4800 = 9800$.\n\nWait, let me double-check the whole approach. We established that $\\left\\lfloor\\frac{149^{151}+151^{149}}{22499}\\right\\rfloor = q_1 + q_2$ where $q_1 = \\frac{149^{150}-1}{151}$ and $q_2 = \\frac{151^{148}-1}{149}$.\n\nThe key step was: $\\frac{N}{22499} = q_1 + q_2 + \\frac{1}{151} + \\frac{1}{149} - \\frac{300}{22499} = q_1 + q_2 + \\frac{300}{22499} - \\frac{300}{22499} = q_1 + q_2$.\n\nThis is exactly an integer, so the floor is $q_1 + q_2$. \n\nAnd we computed $q_1 \\equiv 5000 \\pmod{10000}$ and $q_2 \\equiv 4800 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nSo the answer is $5000 + 4800 = 9800$.\n\nLet me verify the computation of $q_1$ once more. We need $\\frac{149^{150}-1}{151} \\mod 10000$.\n\n$149^{150} \\equiv 755001 \\pmod{1510000}$.\n\n$(149^{150} - 1)/151 \\equiv (755001 - 1)/151 = 755000/151 = 5000 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nCheck: $151 \\times 5000 = 755000$. ✓\n\nAnd $q_2$: $151^{148} \\equiv 715201 \\pmod{1490000}$.\n\n$(151^{148} - 1)/149 = (715201 - 1)/149 = 715200/149 = 4800 \\pmod{10000}$.\n\nCheck: $149 \\times 4800 = 715200$. ✓\n\nSo the answer is $\\boxed{9800}$.\n\nLet me also double-check the $149^{150} \\mod 625 = 1$ computation, since that was crucial.\n\nWe had:\n- $149^2 \\equiv 326$\n- $149^4 \\equiv 26$\n- $149^8 \\equiv 51$\n- $149^{16} \\equiv 101$\n- $149^{32} \\equiv 201$\n- $149^{64} \\equiv 401$\n- $149^{128} \\equiv 176$\n\n$149^{150} = 149^{128} \\times 149^{16} \\times 149^4 \\times 149^2 = 176 \\times 101 \\times 26 \\times 326$.\n\n$176 \\times 101 = 17776 \\equiv 276 \\pmod{625}$.\n$276 \\times 26 = 7176 \\equiv 301 \\pmod{625}$.\n$301 \\times 326 = 98126 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$98126 / 625 = 157.0016$. $625 \\times 157 = 98125$. $98126 - 98125 = 1$. ✓\n\nAnd $149^{150} \\mod 16 = 9$.\n\n$149 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{16}$. $5^2 = 25 \\equiv 9$. $5^4 \\equiv 81 \\equiv 1$. $5^{150} = 5^{148} \\times 5^2 = (5^4)^{37} \\times 9 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$. ✓\n\nCRT: $x \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$, $x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$. $x = 625k + 1$, $625k + 1 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{16}$, $k \\equiv 8 \\pmod{16}$, $x = 5001$. ✓\n\nNow for $151^{148} \\mod 625$:\n\n$151^2 \\equiv 301$. $151^4 \\equiv 301^2 = 90601 \\equiv 601 \\pmod{625}$. $151^8 \\equiv 601^2 = 361201 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{625}$.\n\nLet me verify $361201 \\mod 625$: $625 \\times 577 = 361125$. $361201 - 361125 = 76$. ✓\n\n$151^{16} \\equiv 76^2 = 5776 \\equiv 151 \\pmod{625}$. $5776 - 5625 = 151$. ✓\n\nSo $151^{15} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{625}$. $148 = 9 \\times 15 + 13$. $151^{148} \\equiv 151^{13} \\pmod{625}$.\n\n$151^{13} = 151^8 \\times 151^4 \\times 151 = 76 \\times 601 \\times 151$.\n\n$76 \\times 601 = 45676 \\equiv 51 \\pmod{625}$. $45676 - 45625 = 51$. ✓\n\n$51 \\times 151 = 7701 \\equiv 201 \\pmod{625}$. $7701 - 7500 = 201$. ✓\n\n$151^{148} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$. $151 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{16}$. $7^2 = 49 \\equiv 1$. $7^{148} = (7^2)^{74} \\equiv 1$. ✓\n\nCRT: $x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$, $x \\equiv 201 \\pmod{625}$. $x = 625k + 201$, $625k + 201 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{16}$, $k + 9 \\equiv 1$, $k \\equiv 8 \\pmod{16}$, $x = 5201$. ✓\n\nEverything checks out. The answer is $9800$.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786684868}
